":"The faʿūl pattern names a material consumed in its characteristic use, as fuel is consumed by burning; the author applies this source-material idea to forms such as shukūr and ghafūr.","anchor":"صيغة فعول إذن تدل على المادة التي تُستعمل في الشيء الخاص به","mentions":[]},{"verses":["39:53"],"words":["الْغَفُورُ"],"type":"grammar","speaker":"author","stance":"holds","claim":"Faʿūl has the same form for masculine and feminine and does not take regular sound masculine or feminine plurals; examples include shakūr and ṣubur, shukur, ghufur.","anchor":"وصيغة فعول يستوي فيها المؤنّث والمذكر فنقول رجل شكور وامرأة شكور","mentions":[]},{"verses":["39:53"],"words":["الْغَفُورُ"],"type":"meaning","speaker":"author","stance":"holds","claim":"Ghafūr means wholly characterized by forgiveness, which explains why the verse is called the Qur'an's most hopeful verse.","anchor":"وكذلك كلمة غفور بمعنى كله مغفرة ولذلك قالوا أن أرجى آية في القرآن هي ما جاء في سورة الزُمر","mentions":[]},{"verses":["39:53"],"words":["الْغَفُورُ"],"type":"meaning","speaker":"author","stance":"holds","claim":"Faʿūl is more emphatic than faʿʿāl: ṣabūr gives itself wholly to patience, while ṣabbār denotes a craft or habitual profession.","anchor":"فعول بالتأكيد أكثر مبالغة من فعال ولذلك فكلمة صبور هي أكثر مبالغة","mentions":[]}]}
